Question 1: What happens to everything written to /mlflow-data if you never mount a volume there and just docker run this image standalone? Try it: run the container, register nothing, stop it, remove it, start a new one from the same image — what do you see in the UI?

Without a volume, everything written to /mlflow-data goes into the container's writable layer. This layer is deleted with the container when doing "docker rm". A new container from the same image starts from the image's original contents, so the UI shows no experiments, runs or registered models. If i stop a container, its layer is preserved, but if i remove it, it destroys the data.

