Question 2: Why a named volume here instead of a bind mount to a folder in your repo (the way you might for local dev)? Would a bind mount work just as well?

A bind mount would work functionally, but a named volume is the better choice here. A named volume is managed by Docker, so it doesn't depend on where my project is saved. It behaves the same on Windows and avoids path and permission problems. It's also safer for SQLite, since bind mounts through Docker Desktop's file sharing can be slower and cause file-locking issues on Windows. Because the data lives outside my project folder, I can't accidentally commit it to git. My dataset is versioned with DVC local remote, while mlflow's runtime state shouldn't be versioned by git or DVC, so keeping it in a Docker-managed volume keeps the two clearly separated.

A bind mount is still useful in development because I can open and inspect the files directly, whereas a named volume needs `docker volume inspect` or `docker cp`. Its drawbacks are that it depends on a specific path on my machine, can cause permission problems, may be slower on Docker Desktop, and puts the DB and artifacts inside my repo, so I'd have to gitignore the folder to avoid committing it. For data that must persist between runs, a named volume is the better default.

