Question 11: This compose file is still meant to run on one machine. What would have to change for the inference service to run as three replicas behind a load balancer, or for the mlflow service to survive a machine failure? (You don't need to implement this — just name what Docker Compose can't give you here.)

Compose runs everything on one machine. It can start several copies of a service, but it has no real load balancer, no autoscaling, no rolling updates, and nothing to move containers to another machine if one fails. Running inference with three replicas behind a load balancer needs something like Kubernetes. For mlflow to survive a machine failure, the SQLite file on a local volume is not enough. It would need a separate database such as PostgreSQL, shared storage like S3 for the artifacts, and an orchestrator that can restart it elsewhere.
